====================================================================
A) capitalize(word) -> String        [line 6]
====================================================================
Disassembly at 0x406daa confirms exactly this C++:

    std::string capitalize(const std::string& str) {
        if (str.empty()) return str;
        std::string result = str;
        result[0] = toupper(result[0]);              // char sign-extended to int
        for (size_t i = 1; i < result.size(); ++i)
            result[i] = tolower(result[i]);
        return result;
    }

Key facts:
  * Operates on BYTES (std::string::operator[]), not chars/code points.
  * toupper/tolower are the C-library ones in the **"C" locale**. The binary
    never calls setlocale (verified: no setlocale symbol; identical output
    under LC_ALL=C / en_US.UTF-8 / tr_TR.UTF-8 / de_DE.ISO-8859-1). So the
    mapping is ASCII-only: 'a'..'z' <-> 'A'..'Z'; every other byte (including
    0x80..0xFF and 0x00..0x1F) is left unchanged.
  * The acronyms table is NOT consulted ("HTML" -> "Html").
  * No trimming, no word splitting, no separator handling: only byte 0 is
    upcased, every other byte is downcased.
  * Empty string -> empty string (no panic, no index).

Rust (byte-exact, no panics, no UTF-8 assumptions):

    fn ascii_upper(b: u8) -> u8 { if b.is_ascii_lowercase() { b - 32 } else { b } }
    fn ascii_lower(b: u8) -> u8 { if b.is_ascii_uppercase() { b + 32 } else { b } }

    pub fn capitalize(word: &str) -> String {
        let mut v: Vec<u8> = word.as_bytes().to_vec();
        if v.is_empty() { return String::new(); }
        v[0] = ascii_upper(v[0]);
        for i in 1..v.len() { v[i] = ascii_lower(v[i]); }
        // Safe: only ASCII letters are remapped, so multibyte sequences and
        // their lead/continuation bytes are untouched -> UTF-8 stays valid
        // for valid UTF-8 input.
        unsafe { String::from_utf8_unchecked(v) }
        // (or String::from_utf8(v).unwrap() if input is guaranteed valid UTF-8)
    }

Do NOT use Rust's str::to_lowercase()/char::to_uppercase(): those are
Unicode-aware and would turn "ÉCOLE" into "École" with É upcased/downcased
(and 'ß'->"SS", Turkish dotted I, etc.). The binary leaves all non-ASCII
bytes verbatim: "ÉCOLE" -> "École" only because "COLE"->"cole" while the
two bytes C3 89 of É are untouched; "ábc" -> "ábc" (unchanged, because
lead byte C3 has no uppercase mapping); "İstanbul" -> "İstanbul".

====================================================================
B) truncate(word, length, omission = "...") -> String   [lines 7, 8]
====================================================================
Disassembly at 0x406f98 confirms exactly this C++:

    std::string truncate(const std::string& str, size_t length,
                         const std::string& omission = "...") {
        if (length >= str.size()) return str;
        return str.substr(0, length - omission.size()) + omission;
    }

Key facts:
  * Comparison is on BYTE length (std::string::size()), and the cut is a
    BYTE substr -> output can and does end in the middle of a UTF-8
    sequence. Verified:
        truncate("ééééééé", 5, "...")  -> bytes C3 A9 2E 2E 2E   ("é...")
        truncate("aébcdéfghij",5,"...") -> bytes 61 C3 2E 2E 2E   (lone C3!)
        truncate("日本語のテキストです",5,"...") -> E6 97 2E 2E 2E (lone E6 97)
        truncate("aébcdéfghij",10,"~") -> 61 C3 A9 62 63 64 C3 A9 66 7E (9 bytes + '~')
        truncate("日本語...",10,"~")   -> E6 97 A5 E6 9C AC E8 AA 9E 7E
    So a Rust port MUST work on &[u8] / Vec<u8>; `&word[..n]` on a &str
    panics ("byte index is not a char boundary") for exactly these inputs.
  * NO word-boundary / whitespace awareness whatsoever, and no trimming:
        truncate("hello world foo",5,"...") == "he..."
        truncate("ab cd ef gh ij kl",10,"~") == "ab cd ef ~"   (trailing space kept)
        truncate("abcdefgh   ",10,"~")       == "abcdefgh ~"   (trailing space kept)
        truncate("      ",5,"...")           == "  ..."
  * The omission is appended unconditionally when truncation happens; the
    result length is therefore exactly `length` bytes whenever
    omission.size() <= length (the normal case).
  * Cutoff arithmetic, pinned down by sweeping byte lengths 0..16:
      line 7 = truncate(w, 5, "..."):
        size <= 5  -> w unchanged
        size >= 6  -> first 2 bytes of w + "..."   (5 - 3 = 2)
      line 8 = truncate(w, 10, "~"):
        size <= 10 -> w unchanged
        size >= 11 -> first 9 bytes of w + "~"     (10 - 1 = 9)
  * Empty string -> empty string for both.

Rust:

    pub fn truncate(word: &str, length: usize, omission: &str) -> String {
        let b = word.as_bytes();
        if length >= b.len() { return word.to_string(); }
        // unsigned arithmetic, exactly as in C++:
        let keep = length.wrapping_sub(omission.len());
        // std::string::substr(0, count) clamps count to size():
        let keep = if keep > b.len() { b.len() } else { keep };
        let mut out: Vec<u8> = Vec::with_capacity(keep + omission.len());
        out.extend_from_slice(&b[..keep]);
        out.extend_from_slice(omission.as_bytes());
        // May be invalid UTF-8 (split multibyte sequence) -> keep as bytes,
        // or use String::from_utf8_lossy / from_utf8_unchecked depending on
        // whether you must reproduce the raw bytes the C++ printed.
        unsafe { String::from_utf8_unchecked(out) }
    }
    // Defaults used by the driver:
    //   line 7: truncate(word, 5, "...")
    //   line 8: truncate(word, 10, "~")
    // Library default omission is "..." .

NOTE on the unsigned-underflow branch: if omission.len() > length (e.g.
truncate(w, 2, "...") with size > 2), `length - omission.size()` wraps to a
huge size_t; std::string::substr(0, huge) clamps and returns the WHOLE
string, so the C++ result is `str + omission` (no throw, since pos==0 is
always valid). This path is unreachable through this executable's fixed
(5,"...") / (10,"~") calls, so it is inferred from the disassembly + libstdc++
semantics, not observed. The `wrapping_sub` + clamp above reproduces it.

If the Rust port must byte-exactly emit possibly-invalid UTF-8 (the C++
writes raw bytes to stdout), model outputs as Vec<u8> and write with
io::Write, not as String/println!.

